var e=`# CHAPTER 4 — The Reformatory

I stayed three years.

Everybody talks about the reformatory like it's punishment. For me it was the first time I had a roof, food on schedule, and nothing to do all day.

Nothing to do is dangerous. Nothing to do forces you to think.

And thinking, in there, I found out my father was wrong.

---

He always said the same thing. The world belongs to the strongest. Wanna survive, be strong. And I believed it my whole life, because he was the strongest man I knew — and you already know how he ended up.

In the reformatory there was a kid named Toninho. Sixteen, built like a door, hands that could close around your whole head. Everybody dropped their eyes when Toninho walked by.

And Toninho took orders from a skinny thirteen-year-old called Escrivão. Escrivão didn't hit anybody. Didn't have to. He knew who owed what to who, knew every kid's visiting day, knew which counselor looked the other way for cigarettes and which one for cash. Escrivão thought, and Toninho broke whatever Escrivão pointed at.

That's where, watching those two, it clicked.

Strength takes you up to a certain point on the Hill. Always up to a certain point. After that, whoever climbs is whoever thinks. The brutes are the hands. The hands never own anything.

---

The problem was I was Toninho. I wasn't Escrivão.

I was strong and I wasn't smart. But lying on that bed, staring at the ceiling, I had an idea that changed everything: the brain is a muscle too. If it hurts to grow, then you can force it.

I started with the library. A cabinet with about two hundred chewed-up books nobody ever opened. I read every one. The ones I didn't understand, I read again. Math, history, an anatomy book that became my favorite, an electrician's manual, grammar, an atlas.

And I built a method.

Every week I gave myself a test. My questions, my answers, my grading. Every question I got wrong, my body paid the bill. Ten punches to the face. Ten to the side of the thigh. Ten boards broken over my back, and when there were no boards, the corner of the bed.

Mistake is pain. The body learns that in a week. And what the body learns with pain, the head never forgets again.

In six months I was hardly getting anything wrong. Not because I'd turned into a genius. Because my body was scared of my head.

---

Studying was only half of it.

The other half was organizing that place. I mapped the reformatory the way Escrivão mapped it — who owed, who was scared of who, which counselor could be bought and for how much. In a year I didn't need to hit anybody to be obeyed anymore. Just like Escrivão. Except I was also Toninho, and that changes the math.

Every new kid who came in, I sized up. The ones worth something, I recruited. The ones who weren't, I left for the others. The names of the ones worth something I sent outside, to the Banca, on visiting day.

In my last three months in there I put together something bigger. A plan. A plan for dominance — turf, expansion, a way for the Banca to swallow up pieces of its neighbors without starting a war nobody was going to win.

When I got out, I was ten years old with a notebook full.

And the top brass was waiting for me outside the gate.
`;export{e as default};
